trustprofile: export the approval the producer publishes, and why it withdraws

The outage of 2026-10-25T23:55:17Z and the one of #34 on 2026-10-26 came without a warning: the
approval pinned one SPIRE CA per cluster, SPIRE activated its successor, and every ztunnel refused its
own new certificate. The controller validates every fact needed to forecast that moment, but exports
none of them.

Five gauges, set by IssuerProfileReconciler. The four approval gauges describe the approval of the last
API-acknowledged publication, which consumers pin until the next one; they change only after an
acknowledged write, as cmtc_issuer_profile_acknowledged_valid_until_timestamp_seconds does:

  cmtc_issuer_profile_approval_generation                            the approval's generation; 0 withdrawn
  cmtc_issuer_profile_approval_valid_until_timestamp_seconds         Validated.Until; 0 withdrawn
  cmtc_issuer_profile_approved_issuer_not_before_timestamp_seconds   per approval_cluster and position
  cmtc_issuer_profile_approved_issuer_not_after_timestamp_seconds    per approval_cluster and position

approval_cluster is a cluster ID of the approval, at most 16 (Validate); position is newest or oldest of
its at most two pinned issuers, by notAfter. A withdrawal deletes the issuer series; a cluster that leaves
the approval loses its series without a reset, so no scrape sees an empty family.

  cmtc_issuer_profile_publication_reason{reason}                     1 for the pass's reason, 0 for others

The reason is set on every pass, written or not, from the closed set trustprofile.Reasons: Validated,
SourceInvalid, SourceExpired, SourceDeleted, SourceUnavailable, SourceUIDMismatch, SourceChanged,
GenerationRollback. Every reason is exported at 0 from the start.

observability/v2/rules/proposed/issuer-approval-source-change.rules.yaml turns them into the
CMTCIssuerSuccessionDue/Imminent, CMTCIssuerApprovalExpiring/ExpiryImminent, CMTCIssuerProfileWithdrawn
and CMTCIssuerApprovalGenerationsDiffer alerts; observability/v2/metrics/contract.json defines them.

